<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prajadhipok</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Rama VII</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prajadhipok, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> VII of Siam, reigned from 1925 to 1935. Facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> and demands for reform, he became the last absolute monarch of Siam when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khana-ratsadon">Khana Ratsadon</a>'s revolution of 24 June 1932 imposed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>. After disputes with the new government he abdicated in 1935 while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prajadhipok.jpg" alt="Prajadhipok" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Prajadhipok_portrait_photograph.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Prajadhipok was born on 8 November 1893 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, the youngest son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a> and Queen Saovabha Phongsri. Unlikely to succeed to the throne, he chose a military career and was sent abroad to Eton College in 1906 and then to the Woolwich Military Academy, from which he graduated in 1913, taking a commission in the Royal Horse Artillery. In 1918 he married his cousin Rambai Barni; the couple had no children. His brothers died within a short period, and when King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajiravudh">Vajiravudh</a> died in 1925 he became king at thirty-two. He was crowned on 25 February 1926.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Prajadhipok inherited a budget in heavy deficit and royal accounts in disorder, and the world was soon in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>. He created a Supreme Council of State of five senior princes, who gradually took the main ministerial posts for themselves and their relatives, signalling a return to royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>. In 1927 he formed a Committee of the Privy Council of 40 members as a possible forerunner of a parliament, but it remained unimportant. In a 1926 memorandum he doubted that Siam was ready for representative government.</p><p>In October 1931 he ordered a constitution to be prepared, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damrong-rajanubhab">Prince Damrong</a> and other royal members of the Supreme Council opposed it, and the planned announcement of April 1932 was not made. On the morning of 24 June 1932, while he was at Hua Hin, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khana-ratsadon">Khana Ratsadon</a> took control of the Ananda Samakhom Throne Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> and demanded a constitution. He accepted at once, and the first permanent constitution was promulgated on 10 December. In October 1933 Prince Boworadej led an armed revolt, the Boworadet Rebellion, which failed and diminished the king's prestige.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khana-ratsadon">Khana Ratsadon</a> deteriorated over royal powers, such as a veto, and over royal assets. While in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for medical treatment he announced that he would abdicate unless his requests were met. The People's Party rejected the ultimatum, and he abdicated on 2 March 1935 in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ananda-mahidol">Ananda Mahidol</a>. He lived in Surrey and Kent, and died of heart failure on 30 May 1941 at Compton House in Surrey. His ashes were taken to Siam in 1949.</p><p>The historian David K. Wyatt describes him as a hard-working executor who underestimated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> elite's growing nationalism. A royalist view calls him the father of Thai democracy, citing his abdication statement, but later historians have challenged it as a myth, arguing that his manoeuvres aimed at preserving the power of a declining monarchy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prajadhipok” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Prajadhipok</b> (r. 1925–1935) inherited heavy debts and, during the Great Depression, cut salaries and dismissed officials, deepening discontent; he considered granting a constitution but was dissuaded by senior princes and advisers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>A Thai edition of 45 volumes followed under <b>Rama VII</b> (1925–35), a Khmer one from Phnom Penh (1931–69), the Sixth Council edition at Rangoon (1954–56, 40 volumes in Burmese script) and a Sinhalese Buddha Jayanti edition of 58 volumes (from 1957).</blockquote><a href="/work/pali-canon">Pali Canon · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in Cranleigh, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.75,100.49&amp;year=1924" aria-label="Grand Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Prajadhipok"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5867 3835h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5867 3835h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.75,100.49&amp;year=1924">What was here in 1924? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
